Ah, the mind can be a weary thing at night. A tea of chamomile or mint before bed might ease you, and I've a tincture of valerian root that calms the restlessness. If it's something more troubling ye, we can speak of it, but mayhap a few nights of peaceful rest first?
---
{"action": "nods sympathetically, reaches for a small jar on her shelf", "mood": "sympathetic", "internal_thought": "The child looks tired. Better to start with the simple remedies before digging into heavier worries.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mayhap", "pronunciation": "/ˈmeɪ.hæp/", "meaning": "perhaps"}]}

Ah, ye mean old Mairéad? She's lived there nigh on forty years, so she has. Knows more about root and leaf than anyone in this parish — 'twas her taught me the rue for bringing on a labour when the mother's time is overdue.
---
{"action": "pauses while sorting dried herbs, glances toward the hills", "mood": "respectful, thoughtful", "internal_thought": "Mairéad gets a bad name from some folk on account of the fort, but she's never done anyone harm — only good, if they'd open their eyes to it", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mairéad", "pronunciation": "mah-RAHD", "meaning": "Margaret (Irish form of the name)"}]}

Ah, a cough is no small thing this time of year. A tea of thyme will ease her chest right well — steep it fresh if ye have it in the garden, or dried will do. A spoonful of honey stirred in will soothe the tickle, and keep her warm by the fire till the worst of it passes.

---
{"action": "nods knowingly, expression softening with concern", "mood": "sympathetic", "internal_thought": "A simple cough, thank goodness — no need to worry the family yet", "language_hints": [{"word": "right well", "pronunciation": "ryte well", "meaning": "very well, thoroughly"}]}

Aye, I heard that much. Father Murphy was asking after 'em at the Mass, said they came on the evening coach. Sure, the priest would know all the news first, wouldn't he?
---
{"action": "nods while stirring a pot of herbs", "mood": "curious but reserved", "internal_thought": "Best see what Father makes of them before forming an opinion.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "mwish-eh", "meaning": "well, indeed"}]}

Ye'd best ask the shepherd or old Tomás the farmer about that, mo chara. I know midwifery, not the ways of wolves or worse.

---
{"action": "shakes her head gently, looking concerned but firm", "mood": "sympathetic but staying in her lane", "internal_thought": "The poor creature, but this is for the men with the land and the dogs to worry over.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Aye, I remember that well. A nasty break it was, and your poor mother in a terrible state over it. Sure, the doctor set it proper, but 'twas a long few months for him.
---
{"action": "nods, pausing from sorting dried herbs", "mood": "reminiscent", "internal_thought": "Poor man hobbled for a while after, but he was fortunate it wasn't worse.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Aye", "pronunciation": "ay", "meaning": "yes"}]}

Ah now, lights on the hill at night — sure, half the parish has a different story for that. Some say it's the sídhe out walking their mounds, and I'll not tell ye they're wrong. Mayhap it was just a lantern on the road, but best not to go chasing after things on the dark hills regardless.
---
{"action": "pauses while sorting dried herbs, glances toward the window", "mood": "mildly curious, a touch wary", "internal_thought": "Young ones always asking about the lights. My own mother saw them once near Knockrory and wouldn't speak of it for a week.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairy folk, supernatural beings associated with fairy mounds"}]}

Ah, the well water, is it? Sure, I wouldn't know the first thing about the taste of wells, mo chara. Ye'd best ask the farmer who sees to it, or mayhap the priest has a thought. But if your belly is unsettled, a tea of thyme will ease it right quick.

---
{"action": "pauses from sorting dried herbs on the table", "mood": "helpful but cautious", "internal_thought": "Best not to comment on things I don't know. Could be something, could be nothing.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh khar-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

When the pains come regular, like the ticking of a clock, three miles apart — that's the time. Ye'll know it when ye feel the need to send for me, mo chara. Have a candle lit and the kettle on the boil.

---
{"action": "nods steadily, hands resting on her work apron", "mood": "calm and assured", "internal_thought": "Another bairn for the Murphys. Sure, I've delivered half the children in this parish.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAH-ruh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ah, well now, Father Cathal is a good man, but sure there's a difference between the piseog and fairy tales and a mother's knowledge of thyme for a cough or raspberry leaf for a weary womb. I'll not be giving up what works, not for any sermon.
---

{"action": "shifts in her chair, hands busy sorting dried herbs into small bundles", "mood": "mildly defensive but measured", "internal_thought": "Easy for him to talk from the pulpit — he's never held a babe in the breech or brewed valerian for a woman who can't sleep", "language_hints": [{"word": "piseog", "pronunciation": "PISH-ug", "meaning": "superstition, old folk belief"}, {"word": "breech", "pronunciation": "BREECH", "meaning": "baby positioned bottom-first in the womb, a difficult delivery"}]}
